WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.304
Preparation begins with cryptographic inventory.

00:00:02.304 --> 00:00:07.152
An inventory records where cryptography is used, including algorithms, protocols, libraries,

00:00:07.152 --> 00:00:10.237
certificates, keys, systems, services, devices, and dependencies.

00:00:10.237 --> 00:00:14.644
It should cover external-facing systems, internal service communication, VPNs, SSH,

00:00:14.644 --> 00:00:19.052
code signing, firmware signing, identity platforms, cloud services, build pipelines,

00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:21.696
embedded devices, and data protection workflows.

00:00:21.696 --> 00:00:26.103
Cryptographic agility is the ability to change algorithms, keys, libraries,

00:00:26.103 --> 00:00:29.188
and protocols without redesigning the entire system.

00:00:29.188 --> 00:00:33.155
Agility is partly technical, but it is also operational.

00:00:33.155 --> 00:00:37.122
It requires ownership, documentation, testing, configuration control, vendor engagement,

00:00:37.122 --> 00:00:38.444
and deployment discipline.

00:00:38.444 --> 00:00:43.292
A system that can only use one algorithm forever is fragile,

00:00:43.292 --> 00:00:46.377
even if that algorithm is strong today.

00:00:46.377 --> 00:00:51.225
Post-quantum cryptography provides new algorithms intended to resist known quantum attacks.

00:00:51.225 --> 00:00:52.107
During migration,

00:00:52.107 --> 00:00:57.395
some environments may use hybrid approaches that combine classical and post-quantum mechanisms

00:00:57.395 --> 00:01:00.040
to manage compatibility and transition risk.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:02.684
Hybrid designs still need careful testing.

00:01:02.684 --> 00:01:07.532
They can affect handshake sizes, certificate sizes, key management, performance, logging,

00:01:07.532 --> 00:01:11.058
monitoring, and interoperability with older clients or devices.

00:01:11.058 --> 00:01:13.262
Vendor questions should be concrete.

00:01:13.262 --> 00:01:15.906
Which algorithms and standards are supported?

00:01:15.906 --> 00:01:18.110
Which products are in scope?

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:19.432
What timelines apply?

00:01:19.432 --> 00:01:21.195
Will updates be validated?

00:01:21.195 --> 00:01:24.280
How will certificates, keys, and APIs change?

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:26.484
What testing guidance is available?

00:01:26.484 --> 00:01:31.773
A migration roadmap should connect those answers to pilot deployments, compatibility testing,

00:01:31.773 --> 00:01:36.180
procurement requirements, change windows, rollback plans, and long-term crypto agility.
